## 4. USER ROLES AND ACCESS CONTROL

Implement role-based access control (RBAC). Every protected operation must be authorised on the backend.

### Role 1: Student

Students must be able to:

- Register using approved registration information or an institution-approved account provisioning process.
- Log in and securely manage their accounts.
- View and update permitted profile information.
- Submit new complaints.
- Select a complaint category and provide a clear description.
- Upload supporting documents or images within configured limits.
- Receive a unique complaint reference number.
- View their own complaint history and status.
- Read official responses to their complaints.
- Send follow-up messages where the workflow permits.
- Receive status notifications.
- Reopen or request a review of a complaint only where an approved policy permits it.
- View the final resolution and closure date.
- Log out securely.

Students must not be able to view, edit, or delete another student's complaint or private information.

### Role 2: Department Complaint Officer

This role handles routine complaint processing.

Required capabilities:

- View complaints assigned to the officer or authorised departmental queue.
- Review submitted complaint details and attachments.
- Categorise complaints.
- Set or recommend priority according to approved rules.
- Assign complaints to authorised handlers.
- Request clarification or additional evidence from students.
- Add internal investigation notes.
- Send official responses.
- Update permitted workflow statuses.
- Escalate complaints to the Head of Department or another authorised officer.
- Record resolution details.
- View workload and complaint-processing summaries.

Internal notes must be clearly separated from student-visible messages.

### Role 3: Head of Department (HOD)

Provide a management dashboard with capabilities to:

- View departmental complaints according to assigned permissions.
- Review escalated or sensitive complaints.
- Assign or reassign complaint handlers.
- Monitor overdue and unresolved cases.
- Review investigation and resolution recommendations.
- Approve or reject proposed resolutions where required.
- Return cases for further investigation.
- Authorise closure according to departmental policy.
- Review complaint statistics and performance reports.
- Review the audit history of relevant complaints.

The HOD must not be able to silently alter or erase the audit history.

### Role 4: System Administrator

Provide administrative capabilities to:

- Manage user accounts and account status.
- Assign and revoke approved roles.
- Configure complaint categories and workflow settings.
- Configure notification settings.
- Manage system-wide settings.
- Review security events and administrative audit records.
- Manage approved retention and backup settings.
- View system health information.

Administrative privileges must not automatically grant unrestricted access to confidential complaint content unless that access is explicitly authorised by institutional policy.

### Role 5: Optional Read-Only Reviewer

Implement this role only if it is required and approved.

The reviewer may view specifically authorised complaint records and reports but must not modify complaint content, assign cases, or issue official responses.

## 5. STUDENT REGISTRATION AND AUTHENTICATION

Implement secure authentication with:

- Validated registration forms.
- Unique email addresses or approved student identifiers.
- Secure password hashing using Argon2id or bcrypt with appropriate parameters.
- Password reset using time-limited, single-use tokens.
- Session expiry and secure logout.
- Login rate limiting.
- Protection against brute-force attempts.
- Server-side input validation.
- Authentication and account-management audit events.
- Appropriate session-cookie settings if cookie-based authentication is selected.
- CSRF protection where required by the chosen authentication architecture.
- Secure handling of authentication errors.

The system must support a configurable registration policy. If institutional verification is required, implement an approval or verification workflow rather than automatically treating every self-registered account as a verified BUK student.

Do not use real BUK student records in demonstration data.

## 6. COMPLAINT MANAGEMENT MODULE

This is the core module of the application.

### 6.1 Complaint submission

Create a structured complaint submission form containing:

- Automatically generated complaint reference number.
- Student identifier and account details, populated from the authenticated account where appropriate.
- Complaint title.
- Complaint category.
- Complaint description.
- Date submitted.
- Optional supporting documents.
- Relevant course code, level, semester, or academic session where applicable.
- Optional requested outcome.
- Consent or declaration confirming that the information is accurate, where approved.

Do not require a student to enter their name or student identifier repeatedly if the information is already securely available from their authenticated profile.

### 6.2 Complaint categories

Make categories configurable by authorised administrators.

Seed the system with illustrative categories such as:

1. Examination and results.
2. Course registration.
3. Lectures and timetable.
4. Continuous assessment.
5. Project and supervision.
6. Laboratory and workshop facilities.
7. Departmental administration.
8. Academic advising.
9. Student records and documentation.
10. Other departmental matters.

These are proposed categories, not a claim that BUK has formally adopted them.

### 6.3 Complaint reference numbers

Generate unique, non-guessable complaint identifiers.

A readable format may be used, for example:

`MECH-2026-000001`

The year and sequence should be generated safely to prevent duplicate references under concurrent submissions. Do not use sequential complaint numbers as the sole security mechanism.

### 6.4 Complaint status workflow

Implement the following configurable workflow:

- Draft
- Submitted
- Under Initial Review
- Awaiting Student Information
- Assigned for Investigation
- Under Investigation
- Escalated
- Resolution Proposed
- Resolved
- Closed
- Reopened, where authorised
- Rejected, where permitted by policy

Define permitted transitions between statuses. A user must not be allowed to jump arbitrarily from one status to another.

For every status change, record:

- Previous status.
- New status.
- User responsible.
- Timestamp.
- Reason or comment where required.
- Whether the update is visible to the student.

A complaint must not be marked resolved or closed without the required resolution details and permissions.

### 6.5 Complaint assignment and escalation

Allow authorised officers to assign complaints to approved handlers.

Implement:

- Assignment history.
- Reassignment with a recorded reason.
- Escalation to the HOD or another authorised role.
- Priority levels such as Low, Normal, High, and Urgent.
- Configurable service targets and due dates.
- Overdue complaint indicators.
- Escalation history.
- Workload summaries for authorised managers.

Do not invent official departmental response deadlines. Make deadlines configurable and identify them as proposed defaults until the department approves them.

### 6.6 Complaint communication

Provide a secure message thread for each complaint.

Support:

- Student-visible messages.
- Official staff responses.
- Internal staff notes that students cannot see.
- Attachment references where permitted.
- Timestamps and sender identification.
- Read or response indicators where appropriate.

The backend must enforce message visibility. Do not rely on frontend filtering to hide internal notes.

