## 17. IMPLEMENTATION ROADMAP

Work in the following order.

### Phase 1 — Project inspection and planning
Inspect the existing codebase, confirm the architecture, document requirements, establish the role-permission matrix, and design the database.

### Phase 2 — Core foundation
Implement database connectivity, migrations, authentication, session handling, user management, role-based access control, validation, and error handling.

### Phase 3 — Complaint lifecycle
Implement complaint submission, reference generation, categorisation, assignment, status transitions, communication, investigation, escalation, resolution, and closure.

### Phase 4 — Dashboards and reporting
Build student, officer, HOD, and administrator dashboards, search, filters, analytics, and authorised report exports.

### Phase 5 — Notifications and attachments
Implement secure attachment handling and configurable in-app/email notifications.

### Phase 6 — Security and testing
Run automated tests, access-control tests, object-level authorisation tests, workflow tests, and build checks. Correct defects before release.

### Phase 7 — Deployment
Configure the production environment, migrate the database, deploy the application, verify the live workflow, and document rollback and backup procedures.

### Phase 8 — Final handover
Provide the source code, database migrations, configuration template, documentation, test results, deployment instructions, and a list of known limitations.

## 18. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The project is considered functionally complete only when the following conditions have been demonstrated:

- Students can authenticate and submit complaints.
- Each complaint receives a unique reference number.
- Students can track only their own complaints.
- Authorised staff can review, assign, investigate, escalate, and resolve complaints.
- Internal staff notes are not visible to students.
- Complaint status changes follow permitted transitions.
- Role and object-level permissions are enforced server-side.
- Complaint messages and attachments follow the correct access rules.
- Audit records are maintained for significant actions.
- Dashboards and reports use actual database information.
- The application validates inputs and handles errors safely.
- Automated tests are executed and their actual results documented.
- Database migrations and backup procedures are documented.
- The application can be deployed using documented configuration.
- No production credentials are exposed in source code or frontend bundles.
- The final report accurately describes what was implemented, tested, and deployed.

